                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                              %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    d &amp;= p_1^* + p_2^* - \cdot_G^*
                    \\\ &amp; = p_1^* + p_2^* - ( p_1^* + p_2^* + [-,-]^*)
                    \\\ &amp; = - [-,-]^*
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p as it should be for the Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra of a Lie algebra.
          %p
            The infinitesimal reasoning involved in this proof is discussed in (
            %a{:href => "#Kock"}> Kock, section 6.8
            ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+infinity-algebroid+representation"} Lie infinity-algebroid representation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+of+L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} sheaf of L-∞ algebras
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid+cohomology"} ∞-Lie algebroid cohomology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid+valued+differential+forms"} ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential forms
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+theory"} ∞-Chern-Weil theory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The term “Lie
            = succeed "-algebroid\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-algebroid\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NQ-supermanifolds"} NQ-supermanifolds
            and either ignore the relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            or take it more or less for granted.
          %p
            Possibly the first explicit appearance of the idea of
            = succeed "-Lie" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theoretic
            meaning is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavol+Severa"}> Pavol Severa
              ,
              %em Some title containing the words “homotopy” and “symplectic”, e.g. this one
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.SG/0105080"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            which uses “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NQ-supermanifolds"}> NQ-supermanifolds
            ”. Of course, as this article also points out, in hindsight one finds that much of this is already implicit in the much older theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sullivan+model"} Sullivan models
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"}> topological space
              s
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebras"} dg-algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            and as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+groupoids"} ∞-Lie groupoids
            in particular is clear in hindsight, but was possibly first explicitly realized in the above reference. See also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} rational homotopy theory in an (∞,1)-topos
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebras+on+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} function algebras on ∞-stacks
          %p
            The explicit term
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-Lie algebroid
            \/
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              \-algebroid
            as such is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                \:
                %em
                  On
                  = succeed "-Lie" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                (2008) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/schreiber/action.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Schreiber-InfinityLie.pdf", :title => ""} Schreiber-InfinityLie.pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"}> Hisham Sati
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Jim Stasheff
                Section A.1 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Twisted+Differential+String+and+Fivebrane+Structures"} Twisted Differential String and Fivebrane Structures
                %strong 315
                1 (2012) 169-213 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0910.4001"}> arXiv:0910.4001
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00220-012-1510-3"} doi:10.1007/s00220-012-1510-3
          %p The term then appears in
          %ul
            %li
              Andrew James Bruce,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  From
                  = succeed "-algebroids" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi L
                            %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\infty}
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.1389"} arXiv:1007.1389
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0034-4877(11)00010-3"} doi:10.1016/S0034-4877(11)00010-3
          %p The dual monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#CastiglioniCortinas
              J. L. Castiglioni, G. Cortiñas,
              %em Cosimplicial versus DG-rings: a version of the Dold-Kan correspondence
              , J. Pure Appl. Algebra 191 (2004), no. 1-2, 119–142, (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0306289"}> arXiv:math.KT/0306289
              ) .
          %p
            The smooth spaces of infinitesimal simplices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde D(k,n)
            are considered in section 1.2 of
          %ul#Kock
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              %em Synthetic differential geometry of manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "https://tildeweb.au.dk/au76680/SGM-final.pdf"}> pdf
              )
